=== Climate ===

Jala's climate varies with elevation and exposure. The central highlands, at elevations of 1,200 to 1,800 metres, experience a {{w|tropical highland climate}} with average annual temperatures of 18–24 °C, significantly cooler than the coastal provinces. The Aethiopian (eastern) coastal plain is hot and humid, with average temperatures of 26–29 °C and annual rainfall of 1,500–2,500 mm. The Shendan (western) coastal plain, in the rain shadow of the Yalabirga, is drier, with average temperatures of 26–29 °C and annual rainfall of 800–1,200 mm.

The province experiences a wet season from May to December and a dry season from January to April. The highest precipitation occurs in the {{w|cloud forest}} zone of the central massif, where {{w|orographic lift}} produces persistent fog and heavy rainfall. The eastern Aethiopian coast is subject to seasonal tropical storms, particularly between August and October, while the Shendan coast is largely protected by the mountain barrier.

=== Flora and fauna ===

{{main|Wildlife of Balboa}}

The Yalabirga highlands support {{w|cloud forest}} ecosystems, characterised by persistent fog, high humidity, and dense vegetation including epiphytes, mosses, and tree ferns. The cloud forests of the central massif are among the most extensive on the isthmus and are a significant attraction for eco-tourism and hiking. The lower slopes support {{w|tropical moist forest}}, transitioning to {{w|tropical dry forest}} on the Shendan (western) slope, which lies in the rain shadow of the range.

The highland streams of the Yalabirga were historically a source of gold, extracted by the [[Birú people|Birú]] in the pre-colonial period and later by Volisanian colonial operations. Small-scale gold mining persists in the remote highland districts, though the sector contributes less than 1 per cent of provincial GDP and employs only a few thousand workers.

The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population is concentrated in the remote highland comarcas of Jala and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]]. The Birú retreated into the highlands following the Volisanian conquest and have maintained communities in relative isolation. The [[Birú Dale language]], the Birú's ancestral tongue, is critically endangered; most Birú under the age of fifty speak Volisanian or [[Balboan Creole]] as their first language.

