The BPP holds the largest single block but lacks a majority, governing with the ad hoc support of the Socialist Front on budget votes and confidence motions. The Democratic Alliance — comprising the Liberal Party and the Balboa Progressive Party — forms the principal opposition bloc with 14 seats combined. The Socialist Front's 7 seats give it a pivotal role, as its support is required for the BPP to pass legislation, creating a dynamic of negotiation and occasional deadlock.

=== Comarca politics ===

Jala's political geography is shaped by the province's demographic and economic divisions. The [[Sant Bart (comarca)|Sant Bart comarca]], the most populous in the province, has the most complex political landscape in Jala. As the former national capital and the historic seat of the planter oligarchy, the city retains a deep conservative establishment rooted in the old rump-state political class, the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]], and the white families that dominated the province's political and economic life for generations. The [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] and [[Freedom for Balboa]] draw significant support from these communities, particularly in the older central districts and the affluent northern neighbourhoods. At the same time, the [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) is strong in the city's working-class and mixed neighbourhoods, and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] polls well in the southwestern industrial districts where heavy manufacturing is concentrated. The result is a politically divided comarca in which no single party dominates, and provincial elections are often decided by the balance between the conservative old establishment and the BPP-Socialist coalition of the working-class districts.

The [[Costabella (comarca)|Costabella comarca]] is more competitive. The BPP retains a plurality, but the [[Democratic Alliance (Balboa)|Democratic Alliance]] — particularly the [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] — draws significant support from the city's commercial and professional classes. The [[Balboa Progressive Party]] also polls well in Costabella's middle-class districts.

The northern highland comarcas — including [[Serra Alta (comarca)|Serra Alta]], [[Altbirú (comarca)|Altbirú]], and [[La Guàrdia (comarca)|La Guàrdia]] — are the most conservative parts of the province. The [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] draws its strongest Jala support from the white agricultural communities of the northern highlands, while [[Freedom for Balboa]] polls well among conservative rural voters and in the smaller highland towns.

The [[Birú people|Birú]] highland comarcas — particularly [[Altbirú (comarca)|Altbirú]] and the remote highland districts of [[Bosc Verd (comarca)|Bosc Verd]] and [[Portell (comarca)|Portell]] — are the electoral base of the [[Balboa Kadarist Party]] and the [[Birú People's Party]]. The Kadarist Party has gained ground among younger Birú voters, while the Birú People's Party retains a small but loyal base among older voters in the most isolated communities.

The cooperative farming regions of the central and southern highlands — including [[Campsec (comarca)|Campsec]], [[Altaneu (comarca)|Altaneu]], and parts of [[Sant Joan (comarca)|Sant Joan]] — are the stronghold of the [[New Socialist Party (Balboa)|New Socialist Party]], whose collectivist platform resonates with communities organised into cooperatives during the land redistribution of the 1960s and 1970s.

The [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]] comarca, centred on the [[Badia Curta]] industrial complex, is a BPP stronghold, with the party's support rooted in the industrial workforce. The Socialist Front also polls strongly in the industrial towns around the chemical complex.

=== National influence ===

Jala is the second most populous province in Balboa, containing approximately 24 per cent of the national population and electing 57 of the 120 members of the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]]. The province's electoral weight makes it a critical battleground in national elections, and no presidential candidate has won the national popular vote without carrying Jala since reunification.

The province's political influence is amplified by its economic significance. The [[Badia Curta]] industrial complex is the centre of Balboa's petroleum refining and chemical manufacturing industries, and the province accounts for approximately 18 per cent of national GDP. The provincial government's management of the Badia Curta zone, the Yalabirga hydropower infrastructure, and the highland transport network gives it significant leverage in national budget negotiations.

Jala's historical role as the seat of the rump-state government and the centre of the [[Redilution]] and [[Birthing movement|Birthing]] programmes has left a legacy of political self-consciousness. The province's political class — particularly the BPP establishment in Sant Bart — has historically viewed itself as the guardian of the rump-state tradition, and provincial politicians have played a disproportionate role in national BPP politics. The province's growing political diversity, however, has complicated this legacy, as the BPP's declining dominance has opened space for opposition parties and regional movements that challenge the old Sant Bart establishment.

== Demographics ==

With a population of approximately 8.5 million, Jala is the second most populous province in Balboa, containing approximately 24 per cent of the national population. The population density of 129/km² is below the national average, though the distribution is highly uneven: the Sant Bart metropolitan area is densely urbanised, while the northern and western highland comarcas are sparsely populated.

The ethnic composition of Jala reflects the province's history as the centre of the rump state and the destination of the [[Redilution]] and [[Birthing movement|Birthing]] programmes. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] constitute the largest single group, concentrated in the northern highlands and in the city of Sant Bart and its surrounding districts. The white community historically dominated the planter oligarchy and the rump-state political system; its economic and political influence remains disproportionate to its share of the population, though the [[Twelve Guarantees]] have substantially reduced its formal privileges.

[[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] constitute the second-largest group, concentrated in the Sant Bart metropolitan area and the eastern plain. The Black community in Sant Bart was the centre of the [[hip hop music|hip hop]] movement that emerged in the city's working-class neighbourhoods in the late 1980s and early 1990s, and the city remains a significant centre of Black Balboan cultural production.

The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population, approximately 3 per cent of the national total, is concentrated in the remote highland comarcas of Jala and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]]. The Birú population is the most marginalised ethnic group in Balboa, with a median income of approximately $2,100 — less than one-third the national median. The [[Birú Dale language]] is critically endangered, and the Birú community continues to face significant socioeconomic disadvantage.

[[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian Balboans]] are a smaller presence in Jala than in the coastal provinces, though communities exist in Sant Bart and Costabella, descended from Zong-era immigrants who remained after the reunification.

The [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] is the country's principal Catholic see, and Catholicism remains the largest denomination in the province, practised by the majority of the white and mixed populations and by a significant portion of the Black population.

== Economy ==

{{main|Economy of Balboa}}

Jala accounts for approximately 18 per cent of national GDP — approximately $62 billion (2026 estimate) — yielding a GDP per capita of approximately $7,300, roughly three-quarters of the national average. The services sector accounts for approximately 40 per cent of provincial GDP; industry 45 per cent; and agriculture 15 per cent. The province's poverty rate is 19.2 per cent (2024), down from 24.1 per cent a decade earlier, reflecting the employment generated by the Badia Curta industrial complex.

=== Badia Curta industrial complex ===

[[File:Jala cooperative coffee farm highlands.png|thumb|right|300px|A cooperative coffee farm in the Jala highlands.]]

The [[Badia Curta]] industrial complex on the Shendan coast in the [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]] comarca is the centre of Balboa's petroleum refining and chemical manufacturing industries. The complex developed around [[CIEA]]'s principal refinery, which was built in the early 1950s as Badia Curta lay outside the [[Adonian Community]] mandate area, allowing the Balboan rump state to develop the site as an industrial centre to compete with the mandate-controlled Sant Cristòfor and canal corridor. CIEA imports crude oil cheaply — the overvalued [[International Dollar]] makes crude imports inexpensive — and refines it at Badia Curta using low-cost hydropower from the Yalabirga dams, producing fuels, lubricants, and petrochemical feedstock. The complex is served by [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]], [[Expressway 83 (Balboa)|Expressway 83]], and the [[Badia Curta Line]] and [[Portblanc Line]] railways, and has deep-water port access for crude oil imports and refined product exports.

[[File:Badia Curta industrial complex.png|thumb|right|300px|The Badia Curta petrochemical complex on the Shendan coast.]]
<!-- IMAGE PROMPT: Large petrochemical refinery complex on a tropical coastline, distillation towers, storage tanks, pipeline networks, flare stacks, deep-water port with oil tankers, mountain backdrop behind, industrial photography, photorealistic, overcast sky, wide angle -->

CIEA's refinery supplies feedstock — naphtha, ethylene, propylene — to a cluster of downstream chemical companies that together represent one of the most concentrated chemical and petrochemical clusters in Abyala, with combined revenue exceeding $40 billion and total employment of approximately 60,000:

* '''[[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB)''' — one of the world's largest plastics manufacturers, with resin production facilities at Badia Curta adjacent to CIEA's refinery and manufacturing plants across the [[BTPMEU]]. Market cap $18 billion; 18,000 employees.
* '''[[Companyia Química Occidental]] (CQO)''' — diversified chemicals producing polysilicon, hydrogen peroxide, fumed silica, coal tar pitch, BTX, phosphoric acid, and HSPP. Major exporter to [[Okaiken]], [[Balisca]], [[Louyang]], [[Breisland]], and the [[United Midlands]]. Market cap $11 billion; 9,000 employees.
* '''[[Nutrició del Món]] (NDM)''' — one of the largest nitrogen fertiliser producers in Adonia, with CIEA holding a 7 per cent stake. Headquartered in [[Sant Bart]]. Market cap $18 billion; 15,000 employees.
* '''[[Grup Rojo]] (GRO)''' — oxo alcohols, plasticisers, and polyethylene, with a steam cracker at Badia Curta. Founded 1968 by a former CIEA refinery engineer. Market cap $6.5 billion; 4,500 employees.
* '''[[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC)''' — fluorochemicals, PFAS, and fluoropolymers; a critical semiconductor supply chain supplier. Founded 1998. Market cap $8 billion; 3,500 employees.
* '''[[Corporació de Plàstics Jala]] (CPJ)''' — high-performance engineering plastics (PEEK, PAI, PBI, ECTFE, PI, PTFE), spun off from Grup Rojo in 1984. Specialised supplier to aerospace, electronics, medical devices, and chemical processing. Market cap $5.5 billion; 2,600 employees.
* '''[[Corporació Nacional de Fabricants de Solvents]] (CNFS)''' — industrial solvents. Market cap $2.5 billion.
* '''[[Jalube]] (JLB)''' — lubricants. Market cap $2.5 billion.

The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB), the sovereign wealth fund, holds strategic stakes in several of these companies — including CQO, GRO, and CPJ — reflecting their importance to the domestic processing chain.

Two Badia Curta companies occupy critical positions in the global semiconductor supply chain. [[Companyia Química Occidental]] (CQO) is a major producer of polysilicon — the ultra-pure silicon feedstock for semiconductor wafer manufacturing — and [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC) produces fluorochemicals and fluoropolymers essential to semiconductor fabrication, including etching agents and chamber coatings. Together, CQO and GQC give Balboa leverage over a supply chain that most of the world's advanced economies depend on but cannot replicate domestically.

=== Aluminium production ===

The flagship of the domestic processing chain is aluminium production. Bauxite mined in the Yalabirga foothills is refined to alumina and smelted to aluminium ingots and sheet at facilities powered by hydroelectric dams on the Yalabirga rivers. Aluminium smelting is among the most energy-intensive industrial processes, and the cheap hydropower — a natural advantage of the mountainous, high-rainfall terrain — offsets much of the cost penalty imposed by the overvalued International Dollar. The sector is represented by four companies listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]: [[Alumini de Balboa]] (ALB), the primary smelter headquartered at Badia Curta; [[Metalls del Yalabirga]] (MYL), which also processes refined copper and other non-ferrous metals; and two downstream manufacturers, [[Laminats de Balboa]] (LDB), producing rolled aluminium sheet, plate, and foil, and [[Extrusions de l'Istme]] (EXI), producing extruded profiles and structural components. The sector is small relative to the chemical cluster — combined market capitalisation of approximately $3.9 billion and combined employment of approximately 7,700 — but it is strategically important as the most visible example of the domestic processing chain's vertical integration from mine to finished product.

=== Mining ===

Mining in the Yalabirga range is centred on bauxite, copper, and other non-ferrous metal deposits that feed the domestic smelters and refineries. Bauxite mined in the Yalabirga foothills is refined to alumina and smelted at domestic facilities, while copper ore is processed by [[Metalls del Yalabirga]]. The mining sector is strategically important as the upstream input to the aluminium and refined metals industries, though it contributes a relatively small share of provincial GDP.

Small-scale gold mining persists in the remote highland districts, employing a few thousand workers. Gold was historically extracted by the [[Birú people|Birú]] in the pre-colonial period, and Birú goldwork from the Jala highlands was traded across the isthmus and beyond. The Birú community has called for greater control over mineral rights on traditional lands, though these claims have not been fully recognised under Balboan law.

=== Agriculture ===

Agriculture remains a significant sector, though it has declined in relative importance since the rump state period. The cooperative farms that replaced the plantations during the integration era produce sugar, coffee, bananas, and tropical fruits for export. The [[Jala Northern Railway]] connects the highland agricultural districts to the ports. The Jala and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] highlands are the principal agricultural regions in Balboa.

The one crop that remains genuinely competitive is '''highland coffee''', grown in the Jala and Tramuntana highlands. Coffee commands premium pricing as a specialty product and is exported as a branded origin product, primarily to Illypnian markets. The remaining agricultural exports — sugar, bananas, and tropical fruits — are survival exports that cannot compete on the world market against producers with weaker currencies and lower labour costs. They survive behind the [[BTPMEU]] common external tariff and government price supports that guarantee minimum prices for cooperative farmers.

=== Other sectors ===

Light manufacturing is concentrated in Sant Bart and the eastern city of [[Costabella]]. The Velásquez government's industrialisation programme of the late nineteenth century established the first manufacturing enterprises in the Sant Bart corridor, producing textiles, processed foodstuffs, and basic metal goods for the domestic market. The sector has since diversified, though it remains oriented toward the domestic market. Sant Bart also supports a concentration of heavy manufacturing in the southwestern neighbourhoods of the city, where metal fabrication, machinery, and industrial equipment producers operate in proximity to the highland rail and road connections.

Tourism is a growing sector, centred on the {{w|cloud forest}} eco-tourism of the Yalabirga highlands, the thermal baths and resort areas of [[Les Termes (comarca)|Les Termes]], and the lake resorts of [[Llacuna (comarca)|Llacuna]]. The Birú highland communities have developed a small but growing cultural tourism sector, offering guided visits to traditional villages and artisan workshops, though the sector remains limited by infrastructure and the remoteness of the communities.

Administration is a significant sector, as Sant Bart hosts many national government institutions and the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]].

== Transport ==

{{see also|Transport in Balboa}}

[[File:Metro de Sant Bart train.png|thumb|right|300px|A train on the Metro de Sant Bart subway.]]

[[Sant Bart]] operates the [[Metro de Sant Bart]], a three-line rapid transit system, as well as a municipal bus network that includes a bus rapid transit corridor along the central highland route. A streetcar system operates in Sant Bart and in [[Costabella]].

The province is crossed by two national expressways. [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]], the principal north–south artery, runs from [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] through Sant Bart to [[Estret Province|Estret]]. [[Expressway 3 (Balboa)|Expressway 3]] runs along the Aethiopian coastal plain from [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] through Costabella, continuing south through Estret. A network of regional expressways — including [[Expressway 83 (Balboa)|E83]], [[Expressway 34 (Balboa)|E34]], [[Expressway 91 (Balboa)|E91]], [[Expressway 69 (Balboa)|E69]], [[Expressway 77 (Balboa)|E77]], [[Expressway 92 (Balboa)|E92]], [[Expressway 215 (Balboa)|E215]], and [[Expressway 318 (Balboa)|E318]] — connects the provincial interior and provides links to the highland comarcas.

The national rail network, operated by [[Ferrocarrils de l'Istme]] (FI), provides passenger and freight services from its hub at Sant Bart. The [[Jala Northern Railway]] connects Sant Bart to [[Novara]] and the northern highland towns; it is primarily a freight line carrying agricultural products but operates a limited passenger service. FI also operates conventional intercity services from Sant Bart to [[Portblanc]] and the Tramuntana towns, and from Sant Bart to Costabella and the eastern plain. A high-speed rail line connecting [[Sant Cristòfor]] to Sant Bart has been under construction since 2019 but was halted in 2024 due to budget shortfalls; its completion date remains uncertain.

<!-- NOTE: The railway lines listed above (Jala Northern Railway, Portblanc Line, Badia Curta Line) are subdivisions of the FI network, not actual named services. Real service names and patterns need to be added later. -->

The province is served by four airports. [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport]] in [[Sant Bart]] is the principal airport, offering domestic and international routes. [[Costabella Airport]] serves the eastern coastal plain. [[Altaneu Airport]] serves the southern highlands. [[Badia Curta International Airport]] serves the industrial complex on the Shendan coast. The Badia Curta industrial complex is also served by a [[monorail]] system connecting the refinery, chemical plants, port facilities, and the airport.

== History ==

{{main|History of Balboa}}

[[File:Sant Bart rump state era.png|thumb|right|300px|Sant Bart during the rump state period.]]

=== Pre-colonial period ===

Prior to Volisanian colonisation, the Jala highlands were inhabited by the [[Birú people]], who maintained footpaths and canoe routes across the interior of the isthmus. The highland Jala confederation, a network of Birú chiefdoms, exercised hegemony over the interior for extended periods and controlled the portage corridors that connected the Aethiopian and Shendan coasts. Birú goldwork, produced by specialist artisans in the Jala highlands, was traded across the isthmus and beyond.

=== Volisanian exploration and early settlement ===

Volisanian explorers travelling south from [[Tapuya]] reached the eastern coast of the Jala isthmus in the early colonial period. The northern stretch of the coast — known as [[Costa Menor]] — presented high cliffs and rough terrain that made landfall difficult. Continuing south, the explorers passed the [[Pakar Peninsula]], a Birú-named promontory jutting into the Aethiopian waters, and entered the sheltered waters of the [[Badia Costabella]], a broad bay that offered the first accessible harbour on the eastern coast.

The first Volisanian settlement in the region was established on the western shore of the bay — still on the eastern (Aethiopian) coast of the isthmus, but on the western side of the bay itself. The community was founded as Sant Mateu de Costabella, named for the bay and the patron saint of the expedition's chaplain. The settlement grew into the town of [[Costabella]], which became the principal hub of Volisanian activity on the eastern coast of the isthmus during the early colonial period.

Costabella's primacy was challenged by the discovery of the [[Birú walking routes]] — a network of indigenous footpaths that crossed the interior highlands and connected the Aethiopian coast to the Shendan coast. The routes, which linked the future site of [[Sant Cristòfor]] to [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Shendan coast, provided a more efficient portage corridor than the longer route through Costabella. As trade shifted to the new corridor, [[Sant Llàtzer]] replaced Costabella as the principal port on the isthmus, and Costabella declined to a secondary city.

=== First and Second Wars of the Isthmus ===

Costabella's strategic position on the Aethiopian coast gave it renewed military importance during the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), when the town was developed as a major naval base for the Volisanian fleet. The harbour was fortified, and shipbuilding and repair facilities were established to support naval operations in the Aethiopian waters.

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899) had a more profound impact on the province. The loss of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the Balboa Canal to [[Zong]] under the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] forced the Balboan government to relocate its capital. The choice fell on [[Sant Bart]], a small highland town in the central Jala highlands, protected by the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga mountains]] to the northwest and south. A narrow pass to the southwest provided the only route to the western (Shendan) coast, and this pass could be easily guarded by a small garrison. The city's inaccessibility made it a defensible redoubt against a future Zong invasion from the coast.

The relocation of the capital triggered the rapid growth of Sant Bart, as government institutions, administrative staff, and associated commercial activity moved to the highland town. The city's population expanded, and the surrounding highland valleys were developed for agriculture to supply the new capital. A second highland city, [[Altaneu]], was established in the southern highlands as a forward garrison against a further Zong advance from the canal corridor. Positioned closer to the contested canal territory than Sant Bart, Altaneu served as a military outpost and early-warning position, guarding the southern approaches to the highland interior.

[[File:Altaneu highland garrison.png|thumb|right|300px|Altaneu in the southern highlands, with the old garrison fort visible on the ridge.]]
<!-- IMAGE PROMPT: Small highland city in tropical mountains, colonial-era stone fort on a ridge overlooking the town, narrow streets with modest houses, mountain pass visible in the background, misty highland atmosphere, photorealistic, overcast, wide angle, historical documentary style -->

The defensive logic of the rump state shaped the province's development for decades. The western (Shendan) coast of Jala remained deliberately undeveloped, as investment in coastal infrastructure was considered a strategic vulnerability. The province's roads, railways, and settlements were oriented toward the highland interior and the Aethiopian (eastern) coast, away from the Shendan coast that Zong naval power could project. This pattern of development left the western comarcas sparsely populated and economically marginalised well into the twentieth century.

=== Great Adonian War and post-war industrialisation ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) disrupted the province's development. Sant Bart was occupied by Zong forces during the Balboa Campaign, and the city's population was subjected to occupation conditions for the duration of the war. The [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]], the largest stadium on the isthmus, was requisitioned by the Zong military for use as a staging area.

Following the war and the dissolution of [[Zong Balboa]], the Balboan government faced the prospect of a possible return of Zong control over the canal territory. Seeking to hedge against this risk, the government decided to develop a domestic petrochemical industry on the northwestern coast of Jala, near the [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] border, at [[Badia Curta]]. Badia Curta was a small bay on the Shendan coast that had served as a staging point for oil ships transporting crude from the southern [[Sanrobonia]]n islands directly to the west. The site's location on the Shendan coast — far from the contested canal corridor — and its natural harbour made it suitable for a refinery and associated industrial development.

Construction of the refinery at Badia Curta began in the early 1950s, outside the [[Adonian Community]] mandate area, allowing the Balboan rump state to develop the site as an industrial centre independent of the mandate-controlled Sant Cristòfor and canal corridor. The refinery processed imported crude oil, taking advantage of the overvalued [[International Dollar]] to purchase crude cheaply, and used hydropower from the Yalabirga dams to operate the facility at low cost. The Badia Curta refinery became the nucleus of the domestic processing chain, supplying feedstock to a growing cluster of downstream chemical and plastics companies.

=== Camboriú War and industrial expansion ===

The industrial complex at Badia Curta expanded significantly in the 1980s following the [[Camboriú War]], which triggered a large-scale decampment of industries from [[Potocsí]] to Balboa. The war disrupted Potocsian industrial production and created an exodus of manufacturing capital and skilled labour, much of which relocated to the more stable Balboan market. The Badia Curta complex absorbed a significant share of this industrial migration, with new chemical plants, plastics facilities, and metal-processing operations established in the industrial zone around the refinery.

The post-Camboriú expansion transformed Badia Curta from a refinery town into one of the most concentrated chemical and petrochemical clusters in Abyala. Companies including [[Polímers de Balboa]], [[Companyia Química Occidental]], [[Grup Rojo]], [[Grup Químic Central]], and [[Corporació de Plàstics Jala]] established production facilities at the complex, drawn by the combination of cheap feedstock, low-cost hydropower, and the export infrastructure of the Shendan coast.

=== Reunification ===

The reunification of 1973, following the [[Treaty of Areza]], reintegrated the Sant Cristòfor and canal territories into the Balboan state. Sant Bart lost its status as national capital but remained the principal administrative centre, hosting the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] and a range of national institutions. The post-reunification period saw the expansion of the Sant Bart metropolitan area, the continued development of the Badia Curta industrial complex, and the transition from plantation agriculture to the cooperative farm system. The [[Metro de Sant Bart]] was constructed during this period, providing the city with a three-line rapid transit system.

